<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hattusa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hattusa was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>, located near present-day Boğazkale in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The site has yielded large archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, monumental gates and temples. It was abandoned and destroyed during the collapse of the Hittite state around the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusa.jpg" alt="Hattusa" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_Gate,_Hattusa_13_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest traces of settlement on the site date from the 6th millennium BCE. Toward the end of the 3rd millennium BCE the Hattian people settled there and called the place Hattush. Assyrian merchants set up a trading post there in the 19th and 18th centuries BCE. A burned layer shows the city was destroyed around 1700 BCE; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta of Kussara</a> took credit and erected an inscribed curse on anyone who resettled it. The city was nevertheless rebuilt. Around 1650 BCE the Hittite king Labarna moved the capital from Neša to Hattusa and took the name Hattusili, the man from Hattusa.</p><p>The capital later moved north to Sapinuwa under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i">Tudhaliya I</a> after attacks by the Kaskians, and south to Tarhuntassa under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>. In the mid-13th century BCE Mursili III returned the seat to Hattusa, where it remained until the end of the Hittite kingdom in the 12th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>At its peak the city covered 1.8 square kilometres, in an inner and an outer part surrounded by massive walls erected under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>, c. 1344 to 1322 BCE. The inner city held a citadel with large administrative buildings and temples, and the royal residence stood on a high ridge called Büyükkale. The outer city to the south had elaborate gateways decorated with reliefs of warriors, lions and sphinxes, and four temples. Modern estimates put the population at around 10,000. Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a> have vanished, leaving only the stone walls of temples and palaces.</p><p>The city was destroyed with the Hittite state around 1200 BCE, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>. Excavations suggest it was abandoned gradually over several decades, and a regional drought has been suggested. The final burning probably came after the royal family, elites and state apparatus had left, and agricultural communities with a different material culture settled in the ruins as early as the 12th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The French archaeologist Charles Texier brought attention to the ruins after his visit in 1834. In 1905 and 1906 Hugo Winckler of the German Oriental Society began excavating the royal fortress. Thousands of tablets were recovered, mostly in the then unreadable Hittite language, and a few Akkadian texts identified the site as Hattusa. Systematic German excavations continue, and the site was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1986.</p><p>About 30,000 clay tablets from Hattusa form the main corpus of Hittite literature, among them official correspondence, contracts, legal codes, cult procedures and oracles. One tablet records the terms of a peace between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> after the Battle of Kadesh, in 1259 or 1258 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hattusa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Old Kingdom emerged around 1650 BCE under Hattusili I, who made <b>Hattusa</b> his capital, a naturally defensible site among the rocky highlands of north-central Anatolia, and campaigned southward into Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city of Arinna, a day's march from <b>Hattusa</b>, was perhaps the major cult centre and certainly that of the major sun goddess, known as the sun goddess of Arinna.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers who spoke Egyptian, Hittite, Hurrian or Canaanite languages corresponded in Akkadian, and scribal schools across the region, from <b>Hattusa</b> in Anatolia to Ugarit on the Syrian coast and Amarna in Egypt, trained scribes to read and write it.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hattusa</b>, the capital of the Hittite Empire, was abandoned and partly burned; the great trading city of Ugarit on the Syrian coast was destroyed and never reoccupied; the Mycenaean palaces of Greece were burned, and with them went the Linear B script.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>A bronze sword of Aegean type, inscribed with Tudhaliya's dedication after the victory over Assuwa, was found at <b>Hattusa</b> in 1991.</blockquote><a href="/state/assuwa">Assuwa</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 17th century BCE Hattusili I of the Hittite Empire crossed the Euphrates, burned Hahhum and carried its gods to <b>Hattusa</b>, claiming that he had surpassed Sargon of Akkad.</blockquote><a href="/state/hahhum">Hahhum</a></li></ul></section>
